    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper reports language-task performance for 1B and 3B models, bearing on downstream performance versus scale, but does not reproduce or fit the original pretraining-loss scaling measurements.",
    "gate_note": "The paper cites Kaplan et al.'s scaling laws as motivation but does not measure held-out pretraining loss against parameters, tokens, or compute, fit scaling laws, test compute-optimal allocation, or evaluate architecture-shape effects. Table 4 compares downstream language benchmarks across model sizes; those results are recorded for V6. Other TTS, tokenizer, latency, and speech-quality results do not test a claim version.",
    "test_reason": "This is a TTS and spoken-language-modeling paper that cites Kaplan et al. only as motivation. It fits no scaling relation and does not vary scale in a controlled way. Table 4 reports two sizes per family as system comparisons, not a scale study. TADA-1B and TADA-3B-ML also differ in training data (270K vs 900K hours, multilingual) and context length (1,024 vs 2,048). Two confounded points cannot show that downstream performance improves predictably with scale or loss.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly right but incomplete. The authors conclude that text-synchronous acoustic tokenization gives TTS competitive with the state of the art, with zero content hallucinations on their test sets and much lower real-time factor. They also conclude that Speech Free Guidance brings text-speech language performance close to text-only mode. They draw no scaling-law conclusions.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "No scaling-law fitting or compute-allocation recipe is used; the relevant evidence is a small set of downstream benchmark comparisons across model sizes.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The reported language scores vary by text-only versus text-speech inference mode and by benchmark; the paper also reports separate text-only-guidance results, which are not a size-scaling test.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "TADA-3B-ML scores slightly better than TADA-1B on SI perplexity, sSC and tSC. The 3B model also has more, multilingual data and a longer context.",
        "proposed_claim": "Larger speech-text models initialized from larger LLMs perform better on spoken language benchmarks (confounded two-point comparison).",
        "row_ids": [
          "r7",
